Real-Time Adaptive Heart Disease Detection Framework
Complete Setup, Testing, and Technical Explanation

Author: Thesis Project Team
Version: 1.0

============================================================
1) PROJECT OVERVIEW
============================================================

This project implements an adaptive and real-time heart disease detection workflow.
It supports:

1. Data preprocessing pipeline
2. Baseline static model training
3. Online adaptive learning with concept-drift detectors
4. Automated experiment reports (JSON/CSV/PNG)
5. Dashboard monitoring (Streamlit)
6. API scaffold (FastAPI)

Main goal:
Build a system that does not remain static. Instead, it can keep learning from new
data and track performance under changing data conditions.


============================================================
2) HOW TO CLONE AND SETUP ON YOUR LOCAL SYSTEM
============================================================

Prerequisites:
- Git
- Python 3.10+ (recommended 3.11 or 3.12)
- pip

Step 1: Clone repository

    git clone https://github.com/ameerhamzabutt555/Real-Time-Adaptive-Heart-Disease-Detection-Framework.git
    cd Real-Time-Adaptive-Heart-Disease-Detection-Framework

Step 2: (Recommended) Create virtual environment

Linux / macOS:
    python3 -m venv .venv
    source .venv/bin/activate

Windows PowerShell:
    py -m venv .venv
    .\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1

Step 3: Install dependencies

Option A (recommended in this repo):
    python -m pip install -e .

Option B (if editable install fails):
    python -m pip install numpy pandas scikit-learn river joblib matplotlib fastapi pydantic "uvicorn[standard]" streamlit pytest httpx

Step 4: Verify setup

    python -m pytest -q

Expected result:
All tests should pass.


============================================================
3) HOW TO RUN COMPLETE PRACTICAL WORKFLOW
============================================================

Put your heart dataset CSV file at:
    data/raw/heart.csv

Then run:

1) Preprocess data:
    make run-pipeline INPUT=data/raw/heart.csv DATA=data/processed/heart_processed.csv

2) Train baseline model:
    make train-baseline DATA=data/processed/heart_processed.csv

3) Run adaptive detector comparison:
    make run-adaptive DATA=data/processed/heart_processed.csv

4) Open dashboard:
    make run-dashboard

5) (Optional) API server:
    make run-api


============================================================
4) HOW TO TEST ALL CODE
============================================================

Run full tests:
    python -m pytest -q

Useful subsets:
- Unit tests only:
    python -m pytest tests/unit -q

- Integration tests only:
    python -m pytest tests/integration -q

Code quality checks:
- Lint:
    make lint

- Format:
    make format


============================================================
5) OUTPUT FILES GENERATED BY WORKFLOW
============================================================

After running the pipeline and models, expected files include:

Baseline:
- models/artifacts/baseline.joblib
- experiments/tracking/baseline_metrics.json

Adaptive:
- experiments/tracking/adaptive_metrics.json
- experiments/tracking/adaptive_progress.csv
- experiments/tracking/adaptive_summary.csv
- experiments/runs/adaptive/plots/adaptive_accuracy_progress.png
- experiments/runs/adaptive/plots/adaptive_detector_comparison.png


============================================================
6) MODULE-BY-MODULE EXPLANATION
============================================================

6.1 Data Modules
------------------------------------------------------------
Path:
- src/heart_disease_rt/data/schema.py
- src/heart_disease_rt/data/preprocess.py

What they do:
- Define canonical feature schema
- Map column aliases from common heart datasets
- Validate required columns
- Fill missing values
- Normalize target label format

Why this is best:
- Real datasets have inconsistent column names and missing values.
- Canonical schema avoids errors in training and inference.
- Reproducible preprocessing ensures fair model comparison.


6.2 Baseline Model Module
------------------------------------------------------------
Path:
- src/heart_disease_rt/models/baseline.py
- scripts/train_baseline.py

Model used:
- Logistic Regression + StandardScaler (scikit-learn pipeline)

Why this model:
- Interpretable and medically acceptable baseline
- Fast training/inference
- Stable on tabular clinical data
- Good benchmark before advanced/adaptive models

Why pipeline approach:
- Prevents feature scaling leakage
- Keeps preprocessing + model unified for production


6.3 Adaptive Model Module
------------------------------------------------------------
Path:
- src/heart_disease_rt/models/adaptive.py
- scripts/run_adaptive_loop.py

Approach:
- Online learning with River logistic regression
- Prequential stream-style updates (predict then learn)

Detectors used:
1. ADWIN
2. DDM
3. Page-Hinkley

Why these detectors:
- They are standard, lightweight, and practical for streaming drift detection.
- ADWIN: adaptive windowing, robust in many streams
- DDM: error-rate based monitoring, simple and effective baseline detector
- Page-Hinkley: good for mean-shift style drift detection

Why detector comparison is best:
- No single detector is always best for every drift pattern.
- Comparative setup gives empirical evidence for thesis results.
- Makes your evaluation scientifically stronger for viva/publication.


6.4 Monitoring and Reporting Module
------------------------------------------------------------
Path:
- src/heart_disease_rt/monitoring/reporting.py

What it does:
- Summarizes detector performance
- Chooses best detector by final accuracy
- Generates plots for thesis/report/dashboard

Why this is best:
- Converts raw experiment outputs into decision-ready artifacts.
- Plots help communicate results in thesis and presentations.
- Repeatable report generation improves reproducibility.


6.5 Dashboard Module
------------------------------------------------------------
Path:
- dashboard/app.py

What it shows:
- Baseline vs adaptive top metrics
- Detector comparison table
- Adaptive progress curve
- Generated plot artifacts

Why this is best:
- Fast visual monitoring for experiments
- Easy demo for supervisor/examiner
- Bridges technical outputs with understandable evidence


6.6 API Module
------------------------------------------------------------
Path:
- api/main.py
- src/heart_disease_rt/serving/*

What it provides:
- /health endpoint
- /predict endpoint scaffold

Why it matters:
- Enables future deployment and integration with external systems
- Keeps project aligned with “real-time framework” objective


============================================================
7) WHY THESE TECHNOLOGIES WERE SELECTED
============================================================

Python:
- Best ecosystem for data science + ML + deployment prototypes

Pandas:
- Standard tabular data handling for clinical datasets

Scikit-learn:
- Reliable baseline ML and preprocessing pipelines

River:
- Purpose-built online learning and drift-aware streaming ML

FastAPI:
- High-performance API framework, clear schema-based design

Streamlit:
- Rapid dashboard development for thesis demos

Matplotlib:
- Reproducible research plots for reports and publications

Pytest:
- Industry-standard testing framework for maintainable code quality


============================================================
8) RECOMMENDED DEMO FLOW FOR SUPERVISOR / VIVA
============================================================

